# `vercel env pull` writes every secret in the project to .env.local, and
# `vercel --prod` uploads the whole working tree. Without this line, one pull
# followed by one deploy would serve STRIPE_SECRET_KEY as a static file.
.env
.env.*

_archive
_playbook
_outreach
# Regression tests. `vercel --prod` uploads the whole working tree, so without
# this line they would be served as public files. Run them locally:
#   node test/api.mjs && node test/admin-screen.mjs && node test/master-screen.mjs
test/
# Operator automation control surface - internal Claude Code config, never deployed.
.claude/
# The six speculative auto-shop demos below are now DEPLOYED on purpose:
# delivery-call-scripts.md hands out their URLs on live calls, and they 404'd.
# They carry <meta name="robots" content="noindex, nofollow"> because they are
# built for businesses that have not agreed to anything yet, so Google must not
# index a site branded with a real business's name, address and phone.

# `vercel env pull --environment=production` writes a file literally named
# `production` holding VERCEL_OIDC_TOKEN and the production secrets. It does
# NOT match the `.env*` lines above, so without this a whole-tree
# `vercel --prod` would serve that token as a static file.
production

# Internal architecture notes and one-off operator scripts. Not part of the site.
ARCHITECTURE_AUDIT.md
ARCHITECTURE_FLOWS.md
PANEL_CAPABILITIES.md
SYSTEM_ARCHITECTURE_INDEX.md
scripts/
